// runtime/root/linux/sysmon.bit — the preemption monitor thread (#2580,
// #1768's remaining half; ../darwin/boot.bit's #2579 is the twin). A new
// sibling file rather than folded into ./boot.bit or ./boottail.bit: both
// were already near the 800-line cap (measured 782 / 758 before this change)
// and adding sysmonRun to either would cross it — the same reason
// ../darwin/boot.bit's own sysmonRun forced the #2882 split there. Same
// module as every other file in this directory (module files share one
// scope), so ./boot.bit's `WORKER_MAX_STEPS` import, ./io.bit's
// `sysNanosleep` import and ./time.bit's `parkMonoNs` import are visible here
// with no re-import (a second import of any of them is E0042 — see
// CLAUDE.md's module-import trap). Named for readability only: the checker
// types every top-level declaration before any function body, so resolution
// across sibling files is order-independent (../darwin/bootworker.bit's
// header, verified 838c1dc0).
//
// An independent OS thread that drives `../../sched`'s `sysmonTick` off the
// wall clock, so a green thread that never reaches a safepoint still gets
// flagged as over budget (`../../sched/preempt.bit`'s own header names
// #2577/#2578 as the two callers that act on the flag this sets; this file
// adds the producer). Started once, in `boottail.bit`'s `boot()` step 6b,
// right after the worker bring-up loop — see that file for the call site.
//
// THE MUTATOR-REGISTRY DECISION IS IDENTICAL TO ../darwin/boot.bit's
// sysmonRun, FOR THE SAME REASON — read that file's header for the full
// argument if this summary is not enough. In short: `@nosplit` end to end
// (this function and every callee — `sysmonTick`, `parkMonoNs`, the raw
// `nanosleep` syscall) means codegen never emits the back-edge
// `bl bit_rt_safepoint` this loop would otherwise take, so this thread never
// registers ITSELF. **IT IS REGISTERED FOR IT SINCE #4060, and the answer is
// the `blocked` contract — see `sysmonRun` below.** This paragraph used to say
// the thread "never claims a World slot" and that
// `gcBlockingBegin`/`gcSyscallBegin` were "deliberately NOT used here" because
// both register first; the thread provider now claims the slot at the child's
// entry, because `threadStart` cannot tell a mutator body from this one and
// this thread rides the same primitive as `workerBody`. `sysmonTick` itself
// touches only its own `@nosplit` atomic words (preempt.bit's own header: "NO
// OS CALLS HERE"), never the GC heap, so there is no live reference this
// thread could ever hold — which is exactly the `blocked` precondition, and it
// was the basis of the old arrangement too. `worldStoppedFor` passes a
// `mBlocked` slot with no acknowledgement and without scanning its stack, the
// same outcome an unclaimed slot produced. The STW rendezvous is therefore
// neither weakened nor bypassed.
//
// THE CLOCK MUST BE `parkMonoNs`, NOT `../os.bit`'s `osMonoNs` — matching
// ../darwin/boot.bit's own finding (#2582's oracle: "peer starved, rounds=1"
// vs "rounds=2" under BIT_GC=stress, which forces every poll live, pointed at
// the clock comparison itself rather than at "ok"). `../../sched`'s
// `preemptStamp` (called at every dispatch, workerrun.bit) stamps with
// `monoNs()`, which on Linux pins `parkMonoNs` (CLOCK_MONOTONIC via
// `clock_gettime`, ../../park/linux/wait.bit) — see ./time.bit's own
// `rtTimeMonoNs`. `sysmonTick`'s `nowNs - started > preemptBudgetNs` silently
// compares two unrelated epochs if the two sides disagree on which clock they
// read.
//
// UNLIKE DARWIN, `parkMonoNs` HERE TAKES A CALLER-OWNED SCRATCH (`*i64`, at
// least 2 words — ../../park/linux/wait.bit's own header explains why: a
// clock reading cannot be cached, so every call needs a fresh buffer). This
// reuses the same 2-word buffer the sleep timespec below already occupies: by
// the time `parkMonoNs` writes it, the nanosleep syscall has already
// consumed its own read of it, and sysmon is the buffer's only ever writer or
// reader, so nothing in it needs to survive from one use to the next.

import { sysmonTick } from "../../sched"

let sysmonSleepReq: [2]i64

// `threadStart`'s `done` word for sysmon (./boottail.bit's `threadStart`
// call, same shape as `startDones`). Never read again after boot arms it —
// sysmon has no green task, is not reaped in `boottail.bit`'s step 8 join
// loop, and simply outlives the program: the kernel reclaims its OS thread at
// `exit()` like any other. A dedicated word rather than a slot in
// `startDones` for the same reason `bootSleepReq` (./boot.bit) is not shared
// with a worker's `wkSleepReq` — sysmon runs concurrently with the boot
// thread's own step-7 wait and with every worker, so it cannot share scratch
// any of them owns.
let sysmonDone: [1]i32

@nosplit fn sysmonRun(arg: *byte): *byte {
  // PERMANENTLY `blocked`, NOT UNREGISTERED (#4060). The provider now claims
  // this thread's mutator slot at its entry (`../../thread/<os>`'s child entry
  // hook), because `threadStart` cannot tell a mutator body from this one — so
  // "never acquire a slot", which is what the header above used to rely on, is
  // no longer reachable from here. `blocked` (ABI.md §5) is the mechanism the
  // ABI already has for the property this thread actually has: it holds NO live
  // Bit reference in any frame, at any point, ever. `worldStoppedFor` passes a
  // `mBlocked` slot with no acknowledgement and WITHOUT scanning its stack
  // (`../../gc/gcworldstop.bit`), which is the same outcome an unclaimed slot
  // produced — so the rendezvous is neither weakened nor bypassed, it is told
  // the truth in a state it already handles.
  //
  // ENTERED ONCE AND NEVER LEFT: there is deliberately no `gcBlockingEnd`.
  // `worldBeginBlocking` is a single atomic store of `mBlocked` and keeps no
  // per-bracket bookkeeping, so an unmatched begin leaks nothing; the boot
  // thread's own use of the same pair around its step-7 sleep is the matched
  // case, and this is the unmatched one on purpose.
  //
  // THE PRECONDITION IS THE WHOLE SAFETY ARGUMENT and it is unchanged from the
  // old arrangement, which depended on exactly the same fact: `sysmonTick`
  // touches only its own `@nosplit` atomic words (`../../sched`'s preempt
  // header: "NO OS CALLS HERE"), never the GC heap, on any path. A live
  // reference held here would have been swept just as surely when this thread
  // was invisible.
  //
  // `gcBlockingBegin` registers first (`currentMutator(0)`), which is now
  // redundant rather than forbidden, and is idempotent. If a stop happens to be
  // pending at this instant it parks with frame 0 and resumes — the identical
  // path the boot thread takes.
  gcBlockingBegin()
  let req = int(ptrOf(sysmonSleepReq))
  let n = 0
  while (n < WORKER_MAX_STEPS) {
    storeWord(req, 0)
    storeWord(req + 8, 2000000) // 2ms: sysmonTick's 10ms budget (preempt.bit) / 5
    let _ = syscall(sysNanosleep(), i64(req), i64(0))
    let _ = sysmonTick(i64(parkMonoNs(ptrOf(sysmonSleepReq))))
    n = n + 1
  }
  return nil
}
